===Names===
The [[Botanical Latin]] species name, ''coccinea'', means bright red and is derived from the Latin word {{Lang|la|coccum}}.{{sfn|Jackson|1977|p=36}} ''Crassula coccinea'' is frequently known by the [[common name]] ''red crassula''.{{sfn|Goldblatt|Manning|2000|p=415}} It is also often called ''klipblom'',{{sfn|Jackson|1977|p=36}} but shares this name with species such as ''Crassula fascicularis''.{{sfn|Clarke|Mackenzie|2007|p=71}} Likewise the uncommon name ''suikerblom'' is found in some sources,{{sfn|Jackson|1977|p=36}} but the similar name ''rooi suikerblom'' is used for the distantly related ''[[Tylecodon grandiflorus]]''.{{sfn|Manning|Goldblatt|1996|p=110}} In gardening texts it is occasionally called ''rock flower'',{{sfn|Smith|Van Wyk|2008|p=134}} but this can also be applied to ''[[Colpias mollis]]'' which is also known as klipblom.{{sfn|Manning|2009|p=462}}

==Range and habitat==
The natural range of the red crassula is in the [[Western Cape]] province of South Africa from the [[Cape Peninsula]] to [[Still Bay]].{{sfn|Goldblatt|Manning|2000|p=415}} It has also become [[Naturalisation (biology)|naturalized]] in two areas of New Zealand, [[Rangitoto Island]] in the [[Auckland]] area and Camp Bay on the [[Banks Peninsula]].{{sfn|Webb|Sykes|Garnock-Jones|2026}}

==Ecology==
Red crassula is pollinated by the [[Table Mountain beauty]] butterfly (''Aeropetes tulbaghia''), which, usually for a butterfly, is attracted to red to orange colored flowers.{{sfn|Patterson-Jones|2003|p=139}}

==Cultivation==
Red crassula has been cultivated for more than three centuries.{{sfn|Curtis|1800}} It is valued by gardeners both for the vivid color of its blooms and for ease of cultivation. Plants do require a sandy soil that is acidic and to be well watered during the local winter season.{{sfn|Smith|Van Wyk|2008|p=134}}

The botanist and horticulturalist [[William Curtis]] wrote of the species in a publication issued in 1800 saying, "We have no doubt but that when this superb species of Crassula was first introduced from the Cape by Prof. Bradley, of Oxford in 1714, it was regarded as a kind of Merveil de la Nature; even now that it is common, we scarcely know any succulent that is superior to it, whether we regard its grandeur, the curious growth of its leaves, or the rich colour of its scarlet blossoms, readily produced on plants of a moderate size."{{sfn|Curtis|1800}}

